 "metadata": {
  "note": "Verbatim DDInter 2.0 slice for issue #268 — real shipped rows, unedited. TWO FAMILIES, each a recorded name claimed EQUALLY by several substances, and they differ in the one way that decides whether a chip may print a row's label. (1) The three gallium rows all carry the rxnorm_name `gallium`, and each display NAME already contains it, so DrugReference.displayLabel appends no synonym and `gallium` is nowhere in what the chip would print: the arm used to publish 'The patient has a recorded allergy to Gallium citrate ga-67.' — a radiodiagnostic the chart never mentions — and DrugReferenceService.lookupByToken picked that row by earliest dataset entry, which carries no clinical meaning. (2) The two penicillin G rows also share one rxnorm_name, but their display names diverge from it, so the label DOES append it and reads 'Benzylpenicillin (penicillin g)' — which quotes the chart, and must go on being printed. Kept as a slice rather than hand-authored so both shapes are the shipped data's and not a fixture's invention."
 },
